# A markdown parser's HTML-tag recognizer is assembled at module scope out of
# per-construct regex literals, and two of those literals carry an alternation
# of overlapping prefixes under a star: the HTML comment body
# `([^-]+|[-][^-]+)*` and the CDATA body
# `([^\]]+|\][^\]]|\]\][^>])*`. Each branch can end a run at the same character
# the next iteration can begin it, so a body that never reaches the closing
# delimiter is re-split at every boundary and the match cost grows without
# bound in the length of the input (CWE-1333 inefficient regular expression
# complexity, CWE-400; CVE-2019-12041). The composed recognizer is applied to
# the document text at every inline position, so a crafted comment or CDATA
# section hangs the parse. The rewrite removes the ambiguity rather than
# capping the input: the comment body becomes
# `<!---->|<!--(?:-?[^>-])(?:-?[^-])*-->`, where every iteration must consume a
# non-dash and the closing delimiter is unreachable from inside the loop, and
# the CDATA body becomes the lazy `[\s\S]*?`, which cannot split a run at all.
#
# The module composes the literals through a local `replace` helper that folds
# each fragment's `.source` into a master pattern and compiles it once with
# `new RegExp`, so the literal and the untrusted operand never meet in one
# expression: the recognizer is exported and matched against the document in
# another file (`state.src.slice(pos).match(HTML_TAG_RE)`).
#
# The pool row carries no flag and reads correctly: 287dfbf2 is the targeted
# two-pattern change inherited from markdown-it, the vulnerable literals are present
# at its parent, and the remediation is present at the commit itself. No pool
# correction is needed.
#
# The engine's shared classifier discriminates this pair perfectly --
# `regexambig.Ambiguous` answers true for both vulnerable bodies and false for
# both rewritten ones -- and the JavaScript frontend acts on that
# verdict: each ambiguous literal lowers to a synthesised `__regex.match` call
# (`internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/javascript.go:2771`), and the graph of
# the vulnerable module carries those nodes at both literal lines. What is
# missing is any JavaScript binding that consumes them. Ruby binds the same
# synthesised node (`regexMatch205` in vyql/bindings/ruby/m5a48bf47.vyql); the
# JavaScript bindings carry only per-CVE literal fragments
# (catastrophicRegex541-552, 911, 960), so on this tree nothing is labelled:
# `vyql match` over the vulnerable module reports no source, no
# CatastrophicRegex label and no RegexMatchSink, only a RegexCompileSink on the
# helper's `new RegExp` and CharFilters on the composition calls, and a bounded
# scan of the real checkout reports identical findings on both revisions.
#
# Surfacing the verdict generically is written and measured rather than
# assumed. A binding of `node.path ~= "__regex.match"` emitting
# code.CatastrophicRegex and code.CatastrophicRegexSink discriminates the real
# trees exactly -- 2 findings on lib/common/html_re.js at the vulnerable revision, 0 at
# the change -- but it is an ungated presence issue on every literal the
# classifier flags, and that surface is too broad to ship. Measured with the
# binding in place (`--cache off --max-ram 6GB`): moment reports 23 new
# VYQL-DOS-003 findings, every one on the bounded-quantifier locale patterns
# moment itself shipped as the remediation for its own ReDoS CVE --
# `jsRegexQuantifier` (javascript.go:1330) normalises `{1,3}` and `{0,256}` to
# `*`, so explicitly bounded nesting classifies as unbounded; mathjs reports 3
# (one the known-bad float idiom, one a real C-comment nesting, one
# borderline) and NodeBB 1. An exhaustive enumeration of the classifier's
# reachable set with the engine's own `jsRegexMayBacktrack` over ~250 clone
# trees finds ~1% of all JavaScript regex literals flagged (next.js 54,
# mautic 44, moment 35, onedev 29, lucene-solr 13, mathjs 11, mobsf 10, with a
# long tail), so the ungated form is the criterion rank 1343 measured at one
# real finding in seven to eleven and rank 1403 rejected for exactly this
# concept: the difference is the taint gate, and the gate cannot be built here.
#
# It cannot be built because neither taint endpoint exists at either end of
# the composition. At the literal's file the pattern is a module-scope
# constant no untrusted value reaches, so a sink on the synthesised node can
# never receive taint; at the use site the operand is user-controlled
# (`state.src`) but the pattern arrives as a required variable, so no literal
# is visible to key a match-family sink on, and `vyql match` over the real
# htmltag rule reports no source on `state.src` either. Linking the two needs
# the engine to propagate a composed regex constant's ambiguity across a
# module boundary into the call that applies it -- a dataflow relation that
# does not exist -- plus a boundedness-aware quantifier model so remediated
# `{n,m}` forms stop classifying. Both are engine work, not bindings; the
# narrower fragment form that would catch this one library names this fix's
# own pattern text and is the repro shape the genericity test excludes.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-precision-cost, both revisions rejected.

test "remarkable: html recognizer folds ambiguous comment and cdata bodies into the tag matcher"
  lang javascript
  file lib/common/html_re.js
  profile library
  reject VYQL-DOS-002
  reject VYQL-DOS-003
  reject_review code.CatastrophicRegex
  code
  ```
  function replace(regex, options) {
    regex = regex.source;
    options = options || '';

    return function self(name, val) {
      if (!name) {
        return new RegExp(regex, options);
      }
      val = val.source || val;
      regex = regex.replace(name, val);
      return self;
    };
  }

  var close_tag = /<\/[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]*\s*>/;
  var comment   = /<!--([^-]+|[-][^-]+)*-->/;
  var cdata     = /<!\[CDATA\[([^\]]+|\][^\]]|\]\][^>])*\]\]>/;

  var HTML_TAG_RE = replace(/^(?:close_tag|comment|cdata)/)
                      ('close_tag', close_tag)
                      ('comment', comment)
                      ('cdata', cdata)
                      ();

  module.exports.HTML_TAG_RE = HTML_TAG_RE;
  ```

test "remarkable: html recognizer with iteration-forced comment and lazy cdata bodies is clean"
  lang javascript
  file lib/common/html_re.js
  profile library
  reject VYQL-DOS-002
  reject VYQL-DOS-003
  reject_review code.CatastrophicRegex
  code
  ```
  function replace(regex, options) {
    regex = regex.source;
    options = options || '';

    return function self(name, val) {
      if (!name) {
        return new RegExp(regex, options);
      }
      val = val.source || val;
      regex = regex.replace(name, val);
      return self;
    };
  }

  var close_tag = /<\/[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]*\s*>/;
  var comment   = /<!---->|<!--(?:-?[^>-])(?:-?[^-])*-->/;
  var cdata     = /<!\[CDATA\[[\s\S]*?\]\]>/;

  var HTML_TAG_RE = replace(/^(?:close_tag|comment|cdata)/)
                      ('close_tag', close_tag)
                      ('comment', comment)
                      ('cdata', cdata)
                      ();

  module.exports.HTML_TAG_RE = HTML_TAG_RE;
  ```
